    before: []  # e6a4
    duplicate_of: []  # e6a4
    not_duplicate_of: []  # e6a4
    pr_ref:  # e6a4
    description: "## Problem\n\nWhen code calls a function outside the analyzed codebase (Go stdlib, Lua builtins, npm\npackages, Java standard library), the analyzer emits a calls edge to a synthetic ID like\ngo:fmt:0-0:Errorf:unresolved or lua:?:0-0:ngx.encode_base64:unresolved. No corresponding\nnode exists in the behavior map. This creates a dangling edge that breaks slice traversal:\nBFS hits the edge, tries to visit the destination node, finds nothing, and stops.\n\nScale of the problem (from independent external bakeoff, March 2026):\n- Vault (Go): 50,701 calls + 24,564 imports to unresolved external targets (56.8% of edges)\n- Kong (Lua): 6,893 calls to builtins (62.6% of edges have missing destinations)\n- Jenkins (Java): <1% missing calls (Java's first-party graph is nearly complete), but\n  16,268 decorated_by edges to missing annotation symbols\n\nForward slices stall after 1-2 hops because every frontier call terminates at a dangling\nreference. This is the single highest-impact issue for Go and Lua repos.\n\n## Proposed design\n\nAdd a concept of \"external boundary node\" to the IR. When an analyzer emits a calls or\nimports edge to a target that doesn't exist as a symbol, create a lightweight boundary node:\n- kind: \"external_function\" or \"external_module\"\n- tier: 3 (external dependency) or inferred from the package path\n- A flag marking it as a boundary (e.g., meta.external_boundary = true)\n- Minimal metadata: just the qualified name and inferred package\n\nThese nodes exist solely as traversal endpoints. Slices that reach them can report \"here's\nwhere you cross into external code\" rather than silently stopping. Graph consumers can\nfilter them out if they only want first-party code.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis affects every language but the fix should be in the IR layer (post-analysis), not in\neach individual analyzer. After all analyzers run and before output is written, a pass\nshould identify dangling edge targets and create boundary nodes for them.\n\nThe Java annotation case is slightly different: decorated_by edges to annotations should\neither (a) create annotation stub nodes or (b) not be emitted when the annotation symbol\ndoesn't exist. This is simpler because annotations are a closed set per project and the fix\ncan be in the Java analyzer directly. See sibling item.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- \"Teach edge consumers to treat unresolved destinations as external boundaries\": This\n  pushes the problem to every consumer (slicer, ranker, centrality, sketch). Fixing at\n  the IR level fixes it once.\n- \"Don't emit edges to external targets\": Loses real dependency information. A function\n  that calls fmt.Errorf genuinely depends on fmt — hiding that dependency is worse than\n  modeling it.\n\n## Related items\n\n- INV-miniz: Edge endpoint resolution invariant (this WI is the fix for that INV)\n- INV-mogud: Go vendored SDK classification (subset — tier classification, not boundary nodes)\n- WI-juviz/WI-kujuf: Lean/Agda type signature edges (would also produce dangling edges\n  to external types; this fix would handle them)\n\nSource: /home/jgstern_agent/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/hypergumbo-v2.1.0-bakeoff-jenkins-kong-vault-20260302.md"  # e6a4
    fields: {}  # e6a4
